// The admitted Windows import surface for x86_64-windows (#3328, epic #3322),
// mirroring machosyms.bit's role for Darwin's libSystem.
//
// Windows publishes no stable syscall ABI, so a Bit-produced .exe cannot be
// fully static the way the Linux ELF output is: every OS-facing call must be a
// dynamic import resolved by the loader from its owning DLL at process start,
// recorded in the PE's import directory (IDT/IAT) — the loader-time
// counterpart of Mach-O's LC_LOAD_DYLIB. A name in NEITHER admitted source
// builds clean and dies at load with no line number, exactly the
// machosyms.bit:12 failure mode.
//
// WHY A TABLE AND NOT THE HOST'S LOADER: this Mac has no kernel32.dll or
// bcrypt.dll to inspect at all, so "read the DLL and see what it exports" is
// not merely unwanted (as it is on Darwin, per machosyms.bit:17-23) but
// IMPOSSIBLE when cross-compiling `--target x86_64-windows` from here. The
// predicate must be a pure function of data the compiler carries, giving the
// same verdict from this Mac as it would from the Windows box
// `mustafa-desktop-win` (epic #3322 comment) or a Linux CI runner — never
// discovered from any host.
//
// SCOPE: admits only what the Stage B runtime tickets (#3330-#3338, epic
// #3322) actually call: process and exit, virtual memory, file and console
// handles, thread creation and TLS, wait/synchronisation, timing, error and
// fault reporting (including #3337's vectored exception handler — the
// ticket's own "roughly" category list omits fault handling by name, but
// #3337 is a real Stage B child and reporting a fault IS reporting an error),
// and OS entropy (#3338's `BCryptGenRandom`, the one entry NOT owned by
// kernel32.dll — see below). An entry nobody calls cannot be verified, so
// nothing is admitted on speculation. The exact list, and the reasoning
// behind each group, is repeated in #3328's ticket comment so a later
// extension is deliberate, not accidental.
//
// FIVE DLLS, NOT ONE — grew to three 2026-08-21 (#3336) after the DOCUMENTATION
// claim below was checked against the real loader and found wrong for three
// entries. `SetThreadStackGuarantee` and most of the table resolve to
// Kernel32.dll per Microsoft's own API reference (learn.microsoft.com,
// `APILocation` metadata). This file used to claim the same was true of
// WaitOnAddress/WakeByAddressSingle/WakeByAddressAll — "each of those pages
// also lists Kernel32.dll itself as a DllExport location" — which is WRONG,
// verified directly on mustafa-desktop-win (Windows 11 Pro, AMD64,
// 10.0.26200.9168) via `GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), name)`:
// all three returned NULL. The first real link+run of a program pulling
// these three (#3336's park/windows provider) failed to LOAD at all —
// `STATUS_ENTRYPOINT_NOT_FOUND` (0xC0000139) — which is how this was found;
// `bit build`/`bit check` cannot catch a wrong-but-plausible DLL name, since
// nothing on this Mac can load a real kernel32.dll to check against (the
// header below, unchanged, explains why a table is the only option at all).
// The SAME probe against `KernelBase.dll` (and its apiset alias
// `api-ms-win-core-synch-l1-2-0.dll`, which resolves to the identical
// address) returned the real, non-null addresses for all three. So these
// three import from `kernelbase.dll`, not `kernel32.dll` — the physical DLL
// the apiset forwards to, verified rather than assumed this time.
// `BCryptGenRandom` is the other exception: Microsoft's own reference for it
// (`req.dll` metadata, checked the same way, and independently confirmed by
// the same GetProcAddress probe — nonzero from bcrypt.dll, NULL from
// kernel32.dll) names `Bcrypt.dll`, not kernel32 — #3338 chose it over the
// legacy `RtlGenRandom`/`SystemFunction036` (advapi32.dll) precisely because
// it is the documented, current API (Microsoft's own words: "New
// applications should use BCryptGenRandom"), so admitting a second DLL here
// buys a real correctness win rather than adding one on speculation.
// `ws2_32.dll` is the fourth: #3339's IOCP netpoller admits `WSAStartup`/
// `WSARecv`/`WSASend`/`WSAGetLastError`, originally sourced from Microsoft's
// documented API reference rather than a hardware probe (no interactive
// Windows access in that ticket). #3405 later probed all four of these plus
// the two `CreateIoCompletionPort`/`GetQueuedCompletionStatusEx` kernel32.dll
// entries the same way #3336 probed WaitOnAddress — GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary
// (dll), name) on mustafa-desktop-win (Windows 11 Pro, AMD64, 10.0.26200.0,
// confirmed native — no WSL_DISTRO_NAME/WSL_INTEROP set, hostname
// MUSTAFA-DESKTOP reached via a distinct SSH host/port from the
// mustafa-desktop-wsl alias) — all six resolved non-null against the DLL
// already named here, so no correction was needed this time.
//
// #3340 adds eight more ws2_32.dll entries for the socket layer built on top
// of #3339's poller: `socket`/`bind`/`listen`/`closesocket`/`setsockopt`/
// `getsockopt`/`shutdown` (the ordinary Winsock surface, same names as
// ../darwin/sock.bit's libSystem twin) and `WSAIoctl` (the ONLY way to
// resolve `AcceptEx`/`ConnectEx` — see runtime/net/windows/sock.bit's header
// for why those two are NOT admitted here at all: `SIO_GET_EXTENSION_
// FUNCTION_POINTER` resolves them by GUID at runtime, and a name obtained
// that way can never appear in a static PE import directory). Documentation-
// sourced (Microsoft's own Winsock reference), not hardware-probed in this
// ticket, matching #3339's own honesty note for its four.
//
// #3589 adds a FIFTH DLL, `iphlpapi.dll`, for exactly one entry:
// `GetAdaptersAddresses` — hostname resolution's nameserver-discovery step
// (runtime/net/windows/dns.bit's `nameserverFromAdapters`). Windows has no
// `/etc/resolv.conf` for ../linux and ../darwin's `netFirstNameserver` to
// read, so this is a genuinely new provider-side API surface, not a name
// this table already covers under another DLL. Documentation-sourced
// (Microsoft's own IP Helper reference), not hardware-probed in this
// ticket, matching #3339's and #3340's own honesty note for their entries.
// `compiler/peimport.bit`'s import-table builder groups an arbitrary number
// of DLLs generically already (`plGroupByDll`'s `PlDllGroups.dlls` is a
// `[]string`, and every consumer loops over its full length with no
// single-DLL assumption), so this needed no linker change. No entry here is
// Aarch64- or ARM64-specific; the target is x86_64-windows only (epic #3322).
//
// Emitting the PE import directory from this table is out of scope for this
// file — that is the linker's job (#3329 and successors). This file produces
// the data and the predicate only.

// One admitted Windows import: `symbol` is the exact name the PE import
// directory records, `dll` is the DLL the loader binds it from.
class PeImport {
  symbol: string,
  dll: string,
}

// True if `symbol` (the undecorated Bit name) is DEFINED by some member of the
// COFF `ar` archive `bytes` — the Windows twin of elflink.bit's archiveDefines
// and machosyms.bit's machoArchiveDefines, and deliberately the same notion of
// "defined": a global-binding ATOM, which is what resolveGlobals keys the
// whole-link table on. A member that merely CALLS the symbol does not define
// it.
//
// Unlike machoArchiveDefines, no decoration step: x64 COFF decorates no C
// symbol names (emitpe.bit's own module comment, pelink.bit's header), so the
// archive holds `bit_rt_map_pages` verbatim for the Bit-visible name of the
// same spelling.
//
// `bytes` is `libbitrt-x86_64-windows.a`, which does not exist on disk yet
// (#3341) — until it does, `readRtArchive` fails before this is ever called
// for a LINKING build (build.bit's own ordering), so the empty-archive branch
// below is exercised only by `bit build -c --emit-obj` builds, which never
// call it at all (§11.7's gate is linking-only). Kept total anyway: an empty
// or malformed archive answers FALSE, never an error, the same collapse
// archiveDefines/machoArchiveDefines make, so "undecidable" stays one caller's
// decision.
fn peArchiveDefines(bytes: []u8, symbol: string): bool {
  if (len(bytes) == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let members = parseArchive(bytes) catch _ {
    return false
  }
  let m = 0
  while (m < len(members)) {
    let mod = readCoffObject(members[m].name, members[m].data) catch _ {
      m = m + 1
      continue
    }
    for atom of mod.atoms {
      if (atom.binding != LoBinding.Global) {
        continue
      }
      if (atom.name == symbol) {
        return true
      }
    }
    m = m + 1
  }
  return false
}

// True if `symbol` is one of the Windows imports Bit admits for
// x86_64-windows. The analogue of machosyms.bit:105's libsystemDefines.
//
// A LINEAR scan, matching machosyms.bit's own choice, for the same reason: the
// table is sorted for readability and for the self-check's count tripwire, not
// so a binary search can rely on it. Unlike machosyms.bit's comment (written
// while #1664 was still open), string ordering itself is not the concern here
// — #1664 fixed `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` on `string` to compare content in both
// compilers (95966d1a) — a linear scan is simply the simpler, sufficiently fast
// choice for a table this size. Equality is exact.
fn windowsImportDefines(symbol: string): bool {
  let table = windowsImports()
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(table)) {
    if (table[i].symbol == symbol) {
      return true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

// The DLL that owns `symbol`, and whether `symbol` is admitted at all — the
// (value, ok) shape Bit map lookups use. Callers that only need the boolean
// verdict should call windowsImportDefines instead of discarding the DLL here.
fn windowsImportDll(symbol: string): (string, bool) {
  let table = windowsImports()
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(table)) {
    if (table[i].symbol == symbol) {
      return table[i].dll, true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return "", false
}

// The admitted Windows import surface, sorted by `symbol` (byte-wise) and
// duplicate-free — checked programmatically in pesymscheck.bit, not by
// inspection. Every entry's `dll` is "kernel32.dll" except `BCryptGenRandom`
// ("bcrypt.dll", #3338), WaitOnAddress/WakeByAddressAll/
// WakeByAddressSingle ("kernelbase.dll", #3336 — see the header comment
// above for why), WSAGetLastError/WSAIoctl/WSARecv/WSASend/WSAStartup/
// bind/closesocket/getsockname/getsockopt/ioctlsocket/listen/recvfrom/
// sendto/setsockopt/shutdown/socket
// ("ws2_32.dll", #3339 and #3340, the latter documentation-sourced), and
// `GetAdaptersAddresses` ("iphlpapi.dll", #3589, documentation-sourced); no
// entry here needs ntdll.dll or advapi32.dll.
fn windowsImports(): []PeImport {
  return [
    PeImport{ symbol: "AddVectoredExceptionHandler", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3923: the bounded os_run pair's race-free tree-kill (see
    // runtime/root/windows/osrun.bit's header) -- assigns the suspended
    // child to a Job Object so a subsequent timeout can kill it and anything
    // it spawns with one TerminateJobObject call. Documented at
    // jobapi2.h, kernel32.dll (Microsoft's own API reference); not
    // hardware-probed, matching this table's own honesty note for
    // documentation-sourced entries.
    PeImport{ symbol: "AssignProcessToJobObject", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "BCryptGenRandom", dll: "bcrypt.dll" },
    // #3590: cancels a specific outstanding OVERLAPPED (AcceptEx/ConnectEx/
    // WSARecv/WSASend) once a deadline-bounded net call's deadline elapses,
    // so the wait actually stops rather than merely returning while the op
    // keeps running. Documented at ioapiset.h, kernel32.dll, same source
    // category as `CreateIoCompletionPort` above -- and independently
    // verified the same way #3405 verified that entry:
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), "CancelIoEx") on
    // mustafa-desktop-win (native, confirmed not WSL) returned a non-null
    // address.
    PeImport{ symbol: "CancelIoEx", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "CloseHandle", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateDirectoryW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateFileW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3339: the IOCP netpoller's port-create and per-handle association
    // calls, originally admitted from kernel32.dll per Microsoft's own
    // reference (ioapiset.h) rather than a hardware probe. #3405 verified via
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), name) on mustafa-desktop-win
    // (native, confirmed not WSL): non-null.
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateIoCompletionPort", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3923: the bounded os_run pair's tree-kill container (see
    // `AssignProcessToJobObject` above). Documented at jobapi2.h,
    // kernel32.dll; not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateJobObjectW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3923: os_run/os_run_test/os_run_bounded/os_run_test_bounded all
    // reach this -- the one call that actually spawns the child process.
    // Documented at processthreadsapi.h, kernel32.dll; not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateProcessW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateThread", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3917: parkSleepNs's sub-1ms wait. Documented at synchapi.h,
    // Kernel32.dll (Microsoft's own API reference) — VERIFY against
    // GetProcAddress on mustafa-desktop-win before trusting this, per
    // #3336's own kernel32-vs-kernelbase lesson two entries above.
    PeImport{ symbol: "CreateWaitableTimerExW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "DeleteFileW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "ExitProcess", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "ExitThread", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "FindClose", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "FindFirstFileW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "FindNextFileW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "FlushFileBuffers", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "FormatMessageW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "FreeEnvironmentStringsW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3589: nameserver discovery for `bit_rt_net_resolve`
    // (runtime/net/windows/dns.bit's `nameserverFromAdapters`) — Windows has
    // no `/etc/resolv.conf`. iphlpapi.dll per Microsoft's own IP Helper
    // reference; a FIFTH admitted DLL, alongside kernel32/bcrypt/
    // kernelbase/ws2_32. Documentation-sourced, not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetAdaptersAddresses", dll: "iphlpapi.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetCommandLineW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetConsoleMode", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3501: `bit_rt_fs_cwd`. Hardware-verified via
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), name) on
    // mustafa-desktop-win (Windows NT 10.0.26200.0, native): non-null,
    // matching Microsoft's own documented DllExport location.
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetCurrentDirectoryW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetCurrentProcess", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetCurrentProcessId", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetCurrentThread", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetCurrentThreadId", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetEnvironmentStringsW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetEnvironmentVariableW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3923: reads a spawned child's own exit code after CreateProcessW,
    // for every os_run* variant. Documented at processthreadsapi.h,
    // kernel32.dll; not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetExitCodeProcess", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetFileAttributesW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetFileSizeEx", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetFileType", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetLastError", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3927: `bit_rt_os_self_exe` (runtime/root/windows/os.bit). Documented
    // at libloaderapi.h, kernel32.dll
    // (learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/libloaderapi/
    // nf-libloaderapi-getmodulefilenamew); not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetModuleFileNameW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3339: the IOCP netpoller's batched drain call (Vista+, ioapiset.h).
    // #3405 verified via GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), name) on
    // mustafa-desktop-win (native, confirmed not WSL): non-null.
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetQueuedCompletionStatusEx", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetStdHandle", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetSystemTimeAsFileTime", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "MoveFileW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "QueryPerformanceCounter", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "QueryPerformanceFrequency", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "ReadConsoleW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "ReadFile", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "RemoveDirectoryW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "RemoveVectoredExceptionHandler", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3923: the bounded os_run pair's race-free tree-kill setup creates
    // the child CREATE_SUSPENDED and resumes it only after job assignment
    // (see runtime/root/windows/osrun.bit's header). Documented at
    // processthreadsapi.h, kernel32.dll; not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "ResumeThread", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3345: walks a call stack via `.pdata`/`.xdata` (RtlVirtualUnwind
    // internally) exactly the way a debugger or crash reporter does — the
    // de-risk proof this ticket's evidence bar requires calls it directly
    // rather than needing an attached debugger. Exported by kernel32.dll
    // since Windows XP (Microsoft's own API reference).
    PeImport{ symbol: "RtlCaptureStackBackTrace", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3376: admission-surface only, no runtime consumer yet. Verified via
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), name) on mustafa-desktop-win
    // (Windows NT 10.0.26200.0, native): non-null, matching Microsoft's own
    // documented DllExport location — unlike the three corrected above.
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetConsoleCtrlHandler", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetConsoleMode", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #4016: `bit_rt_fs_truncate`'s Windows provider -- Win32 has no single
    // "set length" call, so this pairs with the already-admitted
    // `SetFilePointerEx` below (move the position to the target length,
    // then cut here). Documented at fileapi.h, kernel32.dll; not
    // hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetEndOfFile", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetEnvironmentVariableW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetFileAttributesW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetFilePointerEx", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetLastError", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetStdHandle", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetThreadStackGuarantee", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3917: pairs with CreateWaitableTimerExW above. Same synchapi.h,
    // Kernel32.dll documentation source and the same pending hardware
    // verification.
    PeImport{ symbol: "SetWaitableTimer", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #2415: `runtime/gc/windows/gcyield.bit`'s `worldYield` provider — the
    // Windows analogue of Darwin's/Linux's `sched_yield`. Verified via
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), name) on mustafa-desktop-win
    // (Windows NT 10.0.26200.0, native): non-null, matching Microsoft's own
    // documented DllExport location.
    PeImport{ symbol: "SwitchToThread", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3923: kills every process in the bounded os_run pair's tree-kill Job
    // Object on timeout (see `CreateJobObjectW` above). Documented at
    // jobapi2.h, kernel32.dll; not hardware-probed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "TerminateJobObject", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "TerminateProcess", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "TlsAlloc", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "TlsFree", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "TlsGetValue", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "TlsSetValue", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "VirtualAlloc", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "VirtualFree", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "VirtualProtect", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "VirtualQuery", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3339: Winsock init and the IOCP netpoller's zero-byte readiness probe.
    // ws2_32.dll per Microsoft's own reference. A FOURTH admitted DLL,
    // alongside kernel32/bcrypt/kernelbase. #3405 verified via
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("ws2_32.dll"), name) on mustafa-desktop-win
    // (native, confirmed not WSL): non-null for all four entries below.
    PeImport{ symbol: "WSAGetLastError", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    // #3340: the only way to resolve `AcceptEx`/`ConnectEx` — see
    // runtime/net/windows/sock.bit's header for why those two names
    // themselves are never admitted here.
    PeImport{ symbol: "WSAIoctl", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WSARecv", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WSASend", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WSAStartup", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WaitForSingleObject", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WaitOnAddress", dll: "kernelbase.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WakeByAddressAll", dll: "kernelbase.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WakeByAddressSingle", dll: "kernelbase.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WriteConsoleW", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "WriteFile", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
    // #3340: the ordinary Winsock surface the socket layer needs beyond
    // #3339's four — same names as ../darwin/sock.bit's libSystem twin.
    PeImport{ symbol: "bind", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "closesocket", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "getsockname", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "getsockopt", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    // #3340: `netAbiUdpBind`/`Send`/`Recv`'s FIONBIO setup and datagram
    // engine.
    PeImport{ symbol: "ioctlsocket", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "listen", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "recvfrom", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "sendto", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "setsockopt", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "shutdown", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    PeImport{ symbol: "socket", dll: "ws2_32.dll" },
    // #3336: parkSleepNs/parkWait's WaitForSingleObject/WaitOnAddress calls
    // otherwise run at the default ~15.6ms system timer tick (measured on
    // mustafa-desktop-win: 200 one-millisecond WaitForSingleObject calls
    // took 3162ms before this call, 297ms after it — TIMERR_NOERROR == 0
    // both times), which fails a 1ms-granularity ticker by over an order of
    // magnitude. Traditionally documented as winmm.dll, but VERIFIED via
    // GetModuleHandleExW(GET_MODULE_HANDLE_EX_FLAG_FROM_ADDRESS) on the real
    // GetProcAddress(LoadLibrary("kernel32.dll"), "timeBeginPeriod") result:
    // it resolves into C:\WINDOWS\System32\KERNEL32.DLL itself (a genuine
    // forwarder, not a coincidental same-named export), so no fifth DLL is
    // needed.
    PeImport{ symbol: "timeBeginPeriod", dll: "kernel32.dll" },
  ]
}
